WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.240
Today, we are going to look at an incredible new&nbsp;
paper, where we are going to simulate flows around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:18.480
thin shells, rods, the wind blowing at leaves,&nbsp;
airflow through a city, and get this, we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.240
produce spiral vortices around an aircraft,&nbsp;
and even perform some wind tunnel simulations.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.000
Now, many of our previous&nbsp;
videos are about real time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
fluid simulation methods that&nbsp;
are often used in computer games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.560
and others that run for a long time and are&nbsp;
typically used in feature-length movies.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.720
But not this, this work is not for that. This new&nbsp;
paper can simulate difficult coupling effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.080
which means that it can deal with the movement&nbsp;
of air currents around this thin shell.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.440
We can also take a hair brush without&nbsp;
bristles, and have an interesting simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.560
Or even better, add tiny tiny&nbsp;
bristles to its geometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.680
and see how much more of a turbulent flow this&nbsp;
creates. I already love this paper. So good.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.720
Now, onwards to more serious applications.&nbsp;
So why simulate thin shells? What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:22.320
all this useful for? Well, of course, simulating&nbsp;
wind turbines. That is an excellent application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:30.880
we are getting there. Hmm…talking about thin&nbsp;
shells…what else? Of course, aircraft! Now hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.800
on to your papers and marvel at these beautiful&nbsp;
spiral vortices that this simulator can create.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.400
So, is this all for show, or is&nbsp;
this what would really happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.920
in reality? We will take a&nbsp;
closer look at that in a moment.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.880
With that, yes, the authors claim that this&nbsp;
is much more accurate than previous methods in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:02:00.480
its class. Well then, let’s give it a devilishly&nbsp;
difficult test, a real aerodynamic simulation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:07.280
a wind tunnel. In these cases, getting really&nbsp;
accurate results is critical. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.920
here we would like to see that if we were to add&nbsp;
a spoiler to this car, how much of an aerodynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.280
advantage we would get in return. Here are&nbsp;
the results from the real wind tunnel test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:28.400
and now, hold on to your papers and let’s see&nbsp;
how the new method compares. Wow. Goodness! It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:35.200
is not perfect by any means, but seems accurate&nbsp;
enough that we can see the wake flow of the car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.680
clearly enough so that we can make a decision&nbsp;
on that spoiler. You know what, let’s keep it.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:48.560
So we compared the simulation against&nbsp;
reality. Now, let’s compare a simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:56.080
against another simulation. So, against a previous&nbsp;
method. Yes, the new one is significantly more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:03.680
accurate than its predecessor. Why? Because the&nbsp;
previous one introduces significant boundary layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:11.200
separations at the top of the car. The new one&nbsp;
says that this is what will happen in reality, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.840
how do we know? Of course, we check. Yes,&nbsp;
that is indeed right. Absolutely amazing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.880
And note that this work appeared just about&nbsp;
a year ago. So much improvement in so little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.760
time. The pace of progress in computer&nbsp;
graphics research is out of this world!

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:38.720
Okay, so it is accurate. Really accurate. But, we&nbsp;
already have accurate algorithms, so how fast is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:45.840
it? Well, the proper aerodynamic simulations take&nbsp;
from days to weeks to compute, but this airplane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:54.560
simulation took only minutes. How many minutes?&nbsp;
60 minutes to be exact. Wow. And within these 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.960
minutes, the same spiral vortices show up as&nbsp;
the ones in real wind tunnel tests. So good!

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:09.120
But really, how can this be so fast? How is this&nbsp;
even possible? The key to doing all this faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.800
is that the new proposed method is massively&nbsp;
parallel. This means that it can slice up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:21.280
one big computing task into many small ones&nbsp;
that can be taken care of independently. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:27.440
as a result, it takes advantage of our graphics&nbsp;
cards and can squeeze every drop of performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.160
out of it. That is very challenging&nbsp;
for an algorithm of this complexity.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.880
So, where does this put us? Of course, the final&nbsp;
simulation should still be done with the old tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:43.600
just to make sure. However, this new method will&nbsp;
be able to help engineers quickly iterate on early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:50.240
ideas, and only commit to week-long simulations&nbsp;
when absolutely necessary. So, we can go from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:57.280
idea to conclusions in a matter of an hour, not&nbsp;
in a matter of a week. This will be an amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:04.640
time saver so the engineers can try more designs.&nbsp;
Testing more ideas leads to more knowledge. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:11.440
leads to better vehicles, better wind turbines.&nbsp;
Loving it. And all this improvement in just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:18.080
a year. If I didn’t see this with my own eyes, I&nbsp;
could not believe this. What a time to be alive!

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.320
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

